@ 30.1 - Proto-Potou-Akanic-Bantu to Proto-Bantu
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Stewart, John M. (2002), “The potential of proto-Potou-Akanic-Bantu as a pilot Proto-Niger-Congo, and the reconstructions updated”. JALL 23:197 – 224 NB: For at least the first batch of sound changes herein, the sound changes applying to those consonants in #U will also apply in U₂ under the following conditions, as reported by Stewart (2002): If V₂ = V₁ (vowel nasality does not necessarily have to be the same, however), changes affecting the vowels will also affect V₂. If C is an approximant, changes involving a nasalized V₁ will also affect C₂ and V₂.
	ʋ̃ > l̃ / #_
	{ u, ũ } > { i, ı̃ } / #S:[-labial]_
	{ ʋ̃, ɪ̃ } > { ũ, ı̃ } / #N:[-labial]_
	{ i, ı̃, V:[-round] } > { uɪ, uɪ̃, V:[+round] } / Cʷ_
	ʋ̃ > ʋ / #S:[-labial]_
	{ ʄ, C } > { c, S } / #_
	ɠʷ > w / #_V:[-nas]
	ɠʷ > w̃ / #_V:[+nas]
	V:[+nas] > V:[-nas] / #S:[+voice]_
	ɪ̃ > ɛ̃ / #(C)V_C
	{ ɰ, ɰ̃ } > { j, j̃ } / #C_
	{ ɪ, ɪ̃ } > { i, ı̃ } / #(C)V:[-high]C_
	ʋ̃ > m / #(C)V_
	O:[+nas -voiced] > O:[-nas] / #(C)VC_